# Chapter Six — Names and Addresses

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.2. This begins the investigation movement. No interaction branches yet.

## Scene 10 — The first officers

= Present: Henry, Edwin, Bell; then two uniformed officers and Detective Ellis Ward.

= Immediate pressure: Henry has been useful for three minutes. The police must take the scene from him, and he must decide whether being understood as a witness is enough.

EXT. TOLLAND'S MOTOR COURT - NIGHT

The first patrol car enters the motor court without its siren. Two officers step out. One goes to Bell. The other looks through the display glass and reaches for the radio at his shoulder.

HENRY
The lower stage door was locked. Mr. Bell opened it. I went in to see if the man could be helped.

The officer looks at Henry's hands.

OFFICER
What did you touch?

HENRY
The door handle. His neck.

OFFICER
Nothing else?

HENRY
No.

The officer writes it down. He asks Edwin for the stage key and the lift key. Edwin gives him both. Bell gives him the matching stage key from his ring.

The second officer goes upstairs to keep the guests in the Winter Room. Edwin watches his brass key disappear into a paper evidence envelope.

EDWIN
That operates half the building.

OFFICER
Not tonight.

More headlights enter beneath the canopy. An ambulance stops outside the display. Two attendants go in through the stage door with an officer. One kneels where Henry knelt. When he comes out, he speaks quietly to the officer and closes the door again.

Detective Ellis Ward arrives in an unmarked sedan. He is in his early fifties, wearing a brown suit that has outlasted several changes of fashion. He sees Henry before Henry can decide whether to introduce himself.

WARD
Calder.

HENRY
Ward.

WARD
Still in the same office?

HENRY
Same door.

WARD
Good. What did you touch?

HENRY
The stage-door handle. His neck.

WARD
Anything else?

HENRY
No.

Ward looks through the glass. A police photographer is setting up beyond him.

WARD
Who came down in the lift?

HENRY
Paul Ellery and his sister.

WARD
Name.

HENRY
Ruth Ellery. She returned alone with the key.

WARD
What did you see down here?

HENRY
The stage door was locked. The display-access door was not. The access stair reaches the gallery above the body; there is something white on its lower treads. The carton is still on the gallery. There are marks below the open rail and a red call button beside it.

WARD
There was paper in his hand.

HENRY
Typed like a screenplay. I could read one scene heading.

WARD
Did you take it?

HENRY
No.

WARD
Good. Save the dinner for your statement upstairs.

Ward turns to the uniformed officer.

WARD
Nobody uses the lift or the display stair. Find out where that red button rings before anybody disconnects anything.

The officer writes it down.

WARD
Mr. Bell, show me where you stood when you gave Ellery the carton. Mr. Price, I need every key to this window and the name of anybody who had one today.

Henry remains where he is.

Ward looks at him.

WARD
Upstairs.

HENRY
Bell left Ruth and Paul together.

WARD
Bell will tell me.

HENRY
The carton is still above the window.

WARD
I can see it.

The photographer's flash fills the display. For an instant Paul, the chair, and the six mannequins are equally white.

WARD
You were first inside. Now you are a witness. Give your name to the officer and go upstairs.

Henry goes upstairs.

## Scene 11 — The Winter Room

= Present: Henry, Ruth, Lydia, Marian, Robert, Nell; then Edwin and Bell; police moving among them.

= Immediate pressure: The supper guests must stop behaving as a group. Henry must give an account of Ruth without converting his understanding of her into fact.

INT. THE WINTER ROOM - NIGHT

The Winter Room no longer has a single table.

An officer has placed the chairs several feet apart. Another writes names, addresses, telephone numbers, and relationships on a yellow pad. The cook stands near the kitchen door in a white shirt with one sleeve rolled higher than the other.

Nell sees Henry come out of the lift.

NELL
Is he dead?

HENRY
Yes.

Marian takes off her coat and lays it over the back of Nell's chair. She returns to her own before Nell can accept or refuse it.

Lydia takes one step toward Nell. She sees the coat and stops.

Robert picks up the cup nearest him.

OFFICER
Please leave everything where it is.

Robert puts it down. It is not the cup he had been using.

Ruth remains beside the coffee Henry watched for her. She does not touch it.

NELL
Did he have my letter?

HENRY
I didn't search him.

RUTH
It was in his inside pocket when we went down.

The officer looks up from his pad.

OFFICER
Please don't discuss what happened.

Ruth nods. Nell looks at Ruth for the first time since Henry returned.

The police reactivate the service lift. Edwin comes up with another officer. He has no keys now. He goes to the service bar, remembers the cups, and leaves his hands at his sides. Bell is brought up a few minutes later.

The police move them one at a time into the small private dining rooms along the curved wall. Doors close. People come back and sit in the wrong chairs.

Robert's statement is longest. Marian's is shortest. Bell demonstrates the motion of passing a key from one hand to another. Edwin draws two different key shapes on the edge of a store plan. Lydia answers one question twice and is taken back inside.

Ward calls Henry last.

## Scene 12 — Henry's statement

= Present: Henry and Ward. Ruth is intermittently visible beyond the glass panel.

= Immediate pressure: Ward needs observation. Henry is accustomed to being paid for interpretation.

INT. TOLLAND'S, PRIVATE DINING ROOM - NIGHT

The private dining room contains a table for four and a painted view of a harbor. Ward puts Henry on the side facing the painting.

WARD
You were invited socially.

HENRY
Yes.

WARD
By Ruth Ellery.

HENRY
Yes.

WARD
Client?

HENRY
No.

WARD
Friend?

Henry looks through the glass panel. Ruth is speaking to the uniformed officer at the far end of the Winter Room.

HENRY
Yes.

Ward writes the word.

WARD
How well do you know your friend?

HENRY
That isn't a question with a useful answer.

WARD
It is when a brother is dead.

HENRY
We met during a case. She told me the truth when it cost her something. We have seen each other several times since.

WARD
Tonight she asked you to come because?

HENRY
She expected Lydia Shaw to force a private matter into the room. She wanted one person there who would not help them perform it.

WARD
Did she know her brother was invited?

HENRY
Her reaction said no.

WARD
What did she do?

Henry tells him: the place card, the elevator, Paul's wish to leave, Henry's hand on the closing door.

Ward stops him there.

WARD
Why did you stop the door?

HENRY
I don't know.

WARD
You put your hand in a closing door for a man you had never met.

HENRY
Yes.

WARD
And you don't know why.

HENRY
No.

Ward writes it down. Henry watches him write it.

Henry goes on: the first course, June's sentence, the envelope, the carton, the brass key, Paul and Ruth descending, Ruth returning alone.

Ward does not interrupt again until Henry reaches the coffee.

WARD
You said she touched it to her mouth.

HENRY
Yes.

WARD
Did she drink?

HENRY
I don't think so.

WARD
Was she frightened?

HENRY
She didn't drink the coffee.

WARD
That isn't what I asked.

HENRY
It is what I saw.

Ward closes his notebook.

WARD
Keep doing that.

HENRY
Are you holding anyone?

WARD
Not tonight.

He stands.

WARD
You do not go back into the window or the gallery. Do not compare statements with the others tonight. You are one of them.

Henry gets up.

WARD
Calder.

Henry waits.

WARD
If you remember something, call me before you decide what it means.

HENRY
I will.

## Scene 13 — Ruth's account

= Present: Henry and Ruth, after their independent statements.

= Immediate pressure: Ruth needs to know whether Henry described her or protected her. Henry must hear what she offers without turning the conversation into a second statement.

INT. TOLLAND'S, PASSENGER ELEVATORS - NIGHT

It is after one in the morning when the officers finish taking initial accounts. Ward has not announced a theory. The guests may leave after confirming where they can be reached.

Ruth finds Henry beside the dark passenger elevators.

RUTH
What did you tell him?

HENRY
Ward told us not to compare statements.

Ruth looks toward the private room where Ward is speaking with Bell.

RUTH
Mr. Bell gave Paul the carton. Paul handed me the lift key. Then Bell went into receiving. Paul opened the carton. There were typed pages and envelopes. I did not read them.

Henry says nothing.

RUTH
He wanted me to tell Nell something for him. He said, "Help me tell her." I refused. We argued. He said he needed time, and I left him beside the display door.

HENRY
Why are you telling me?

RUTH
Because Ward will. I don't want you deciding what I kept from you before you know what I said. I told him the same thing.

An officer opens the private-room door and calls Ruth back inside. She goes without taking the coffee she left beside Henry.

## Scene 14 — What Paul brought back

= Present: Henry, Nell, Lydia, Marian, Robert, Ruth at a distance.

= Immediate pressure: The police have finished with the guests for tonight. Nell has sat for an hour above Paul's body, and the only person who can tell her what he brought is the one who arranged for him to bring it.

EXT. TOLLAND'S MOTOR COURT - NIGHT

The motor court has been cleared of police cars except for Ward's sedan. A gray screen now covers most of the display glass. Light escapes around its edges. The ambulance has already gone.

Robert holds Marian's coat. Marian remains near Nell without standing beside her. Lydia waits with her unopened suitcase.

Ruth approaches Nell.

RUTH
I can take you home.

NELL
No.

RUTH
I don't think you should be alone.

NELL
Then Marian can come.

Marian does not answer for her.

NELL
If she wants.

MARIAN
I want.

Ruth nods. She moves away toward the service door but does not leave.

Nell looks at Henry.

NELL
What was in the window with him?

HENRY
The carton was on the gallery. There was a torn typed page in his hand.

NELL
And my letter?

HENRY
The police have his clothing. They will tell you what they found.

LYDIA
The carton was meant for you.

Nell turns to her.

NELL
What does that mean?

Lydia looks at Marian. Marian's face gives her no permission and no escape.

LYDIA
I called Paul because he had something that belonged to June.

NELL
My mother.

LYDIA
Yes.

NELL
What?

LYDIA
Something of hers I should have given you years ago. I meant to. There was never a week when it would not have been the wrong week, and then there were fourteen years of wrong weeks, and I am not going to stand in a motor court and pretend that was—

NELL
How did he get it?

LYDIA
I wanted him to answer that.

NELL
You brought Paul here to return it to me.

LYDIA
Yes.

NELL
Without telling me what it was.

LYDIA
Would you have come?

NELL
You already used that excuse tonight.

Lydia lowers her eyes.

Nell turns to Ruth. Ruth has come out from beneath the canopy as far as the edge of the light.

NELL
You went down with him.

RUTH
Yes.

NELL
Did he say anything about me?

RUTH
He said you would listen.

NELL
I would have.

Nobody helps her with the tense.

Marian holds out the coat. Nell takes it and cannot find the second sleeve. She tries twice. Marian turns the coat around and holds it open, and Nell lets her.

Lydia takes one step toward them.

NELL
No.

Lydia stops where she is.

Robert has brought the car under the canopy. Marian and Nell go toward it. At the edge of the light Nell turns back, not to Lydia and not to Ruth.

NELL
Mr. Calder. You told me what you saw.

HENRY
Yes.

NELL
And what you didn't.

HENRY
Yes.

She gets into the car. Robert closes the door for her and gets in without looking at Lydia.

Lydia picks up her suitcase and does not go anywhere with it. Edwin, who has been standing at the service door with no keys in his pockets, takes it from her.

Ruth comes the rest of the way to Henry.

RUTH
I'll find a cab.

HENRY
I'll walk you to Hill.

RUTH
No. Not tonight.

She goes out under the canopy toward the street. He watches her to the corner. She does not look back, and he does not expect her to.

The display screen moves in the night air. Behind it, the white paper moon remains lit.

Henry entered Tolland's with Ruth. He leaves alone.
